.TH MAZE 6 "September 2026" "maze 1.0.0" "Games"
.SH NAME
maze \- simple auto-generated maze game for the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B maze
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B maze
generates a maze, draws it with box-drawing characters and lets you walk a
player from the top-left corner to the exit in the bottom-right corner using
the arrow keys or the WASD keys.
.PP
Each maze is produced by an iterative recursive backtracker, so it is a
.I perfect
maze: there is exactly one route from the start to the exit, the exit is always
reachable, and there are no loops.
.PP
The board starts small (a 9\(mu6 grid of cells) and grows by one cell in each
direction for every level you clear. A status line shows the current level and
board size, the number of moves, the elapsed time, your best run in the session
and a compass pointing towards the exit. Walking into a wall costs nothing;
only moves into an open cell are counted.
.PP
The game is a single bash script with no dependencies beyond
.BR bash (1),
.BR stty (1)
and optionally
.BR tput (1).
It restores the terminal settings before exiting.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-size " CxR"
Use a fixed maze size in cells, given as columns \(mu rows, for example
.BR "\-\-size 12x8" .
The size is reduced if it does not fit the terminal, and boards are capped at
40\(mu24 cells.
.TP
.BI \-\-preset " name"
Starting size:
.BR small " (9\(mu6, the default),"
.B medium " (16\(mu11)"
or
.BR large " (25\(mu17)."
Ignored when
.B \-\-size
is given.
.TP
.BI \-\-seed " n"
Seed the random number generator so the same maze can be replayed. Reproducing
a maze only works within one build of bash, because the sequence produced by
bash's
.B $RANDOM
for a given seed differs between bash 3.2 and bash 5.
.TP
.B \-\-fog
Start with fog of war enabled: only cells near the player are drawn.
.TP
.B \-\-ascii
Use plain ASCII characters instead of box-drawing characters, for terminals or
fonts without Unicode support.
.TP
.B \-\-no-color
Disable ANSI colours. Colours are also disabled automatically when the output
is not a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-print-solution
Print the keystrokes that solve level 1 for the chosen seed and exit. Useful
for scripting and for self-tests.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short usage message and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version, copyright and licence notice and exit.
.SH CONTROLS
.TP
.B arrow keys, W A S D
Move the player.
.TP
.B h
Show or hide a hint path to the exit.
.TP
.B r
Regenerate the maze at the same level and reset the move counter.
.TP
.B n
Skip to a fresh maze, one level on.
.TP
.B f
Toggle fog of war.
.TP
.BR q ", " Esc ", " Ctrl\-C
Quit. The final summary is printed on exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Play a small maze:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maze
.EE
.RE
.PP
Play a fixed 20\(mu12 maze with fog of war:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maze \-\-size 20x12 \-\-fog
.EE
.RE
.PP
Replay the same maze twice:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maze \-\-seed 42
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the solution to level 1 (the keys are w, a, s, d):
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
maze \-\-print-solution \-\-seed 42
.EE
.RE
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Normal exit, including
.B \-\-help
and
.BR \-\-version .
.TP
.B 1
An internal error, such as a maze with no path to the exit.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: an unknown option, an unknown preset, a malformed
.B \-\-size
or a non-numeric
.BR \-\-seed .
.TP
.B 130
Terminated by
.BR SIGINT .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LANG ", " LC_ALL
If the locale name mentions UTF-8, box-drawing characters are used; otherwise
the game falls back to ASCII unless
.B \-\-ascii
is given.
.TP
.B TERM
Used with
.BR tput (1)
to determine the terminal size when
.BR stty (1)
does not report a usable one.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
Not used by the game itself; only its test suite writes there.
.SH FILES
.B maze
writes no files and keeps no configuration. The best score is only remembered
for the current session.
.SH NOTES
The game reads single keystrokes, so it requires a terminal: it puts the
terminal into raw mode on start and restores the previous settings on exit,
including after a signal. Keyboard input can also be piped in, which is how the
test suite drives it.
